body:
A service that holds a `SocketRequest` client hears when its socket opens and closes. Tachyon sends two built-in event models to that service only, never to other services:

| Event model | Fields | When |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SocketOpened` | `path` (text), `reconnected` (true or false) | The socket opened. `reconnected` is false the first time and true after every later open, also after an automatic reconnect. |
| `SocketClosed` | `path` (text), `code` (a number), `reconnecting` (true or false) | The socket closed. `code` is the WebSocket close code. `reconnecting` is true when Tachyon will open the socket again by itself. |

Write a listener with a parameter of the model type, like any `@OnEvent` listener. Do not declare the models in `client/models/`. `path` is the socket path, so a service with two socket clients can tell them apart.

The browser sends no `Authorization` header on a WebSocket. So a socket that needs a signed-in user authenticates with its first message, and must do it again after each reconnect, because the server sees a new connection. Send that message in the `SocketOpened` listener:

```go
// @Service
type RealtimeService struct {
	// @Needs
	realtime *RealtimeClient
	// @Needs
	session *SessionService
	Connected bool
}

// @OnEvent
func (service *RealtimeService) Opened(event SocketOpened) error {
	return service.realtime.Send(RealtimeAuthentication{Token: service.session.AccessToken})
}

// @OnEvent
func (service *RealtimeService) Closed(event SocketClosed) {
	service.Connected = false
}
```

In TypeScript, the same listener is `@OnEvent() opened(event: SocketOpened) { this.realtime.send({ token: this.session.accessToken }) }`. In Python, an `@on_event` listener is synchronous (async `OnEvent` methods are still pending), and `send` is async. So a Python listener cannot send the message itself: it records the state, for example `def opened(self, event: SocketOpened) -> None: self.authenticated = False`, and an `async def` method of the service sends it, such as an `@every` method or the next call from the view.

Messages that the service sent while the socket was closed wait in a queue (at most 16). When the socket opens, Tachyon first runs the `SocketOpened` listeners, then sends the waiting messages. So the authentication message reaches the server first. Send it before any other awaited call in the listener: a message that the service sends while the listener still runs goes out at once.

The socket reconnects after 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 seconds and then stops; close codes 1000, 1003, 1007 and 1009 do not reconnect. After it stops, the next `Send` opens it again, and `SocketOpened` arrives with `reconnected` true. When the component that holds the service unmounts, the socket closes and no event arrives.

The listener is an ordinary `@OnEvent` listener in each Tac service language: JavaScript, TypeScript, Go, Rust, C#, F#, Swift, Kotlin, Java, Dart, Ruby, Lua, Python and C++. Python names the models as built-in event models, as ADR 0121 describes. The events work on every target where the page has `WebSocket`: the web and the macOS, Windows, Linux, iOS and Android apps (ADR 0120).
